***What is being built***

The source can be used to build two components:
- `p4` CLI binary itself
- `Perforce API` 

Both components require OpenSSL (3.x). Depending on how you obtain OpenSSL build (from external source or locally) you may run into additional dependencies at some point.

***Linux***

Tested on WSL/Ubuntu 26.04

== Preparations ==

1. Get `jam`:
- `sudo apt update`
- `sudo apt install jam`

2. Install compiler tools
- `sudo apt install -y build-essential`

3. Enable Linux file metadata in WSL
- Open `/etc/wsl.conf` and add
```
[automount]
options = "metadata"
```
- Restart WSL

4. Get OpenSSL
- `sudo apt-get install libssl-dev`

Current version as of writing this is `3.5.5-1ubuntu3.7` (`apt-cache show libssl-dev`)

== To build `Perforce API`

1. Run `jam -sOSVER=26 -sGCCVER=11.4.0 -sSMARTHEAP=0 -sTYPE=pic -sMALLOC_OVERRIDE=0 -sUSE_EXTENSIONS=0 -sSSLLIBDIR=/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/ -sSSLINCDIR=/usr/include/ -sPRODUCTION=1 p4api.tar` (for Release build)

== To build `P4 CLI`

1. Install additional link-time dependencies

As of writing, the version obtained using `sudo apt-get install libssl-dev` (`3.5.5-1ubuntu3.7` - OpenSSL 3+ with compression) depends on:
- sudo apt install -y libjitterentropy3-dev
- sudo apt install -y libzstd-dev
- sudo apt install -y zlib1g-dev

2. Run `jam -sOSVER=26 -sGCCVER=11.4.0 -sSMARTHEAP=0 -sTYPE=pic -sMALLOC_OVERRIDE=0 -sUSE_EXTENSIONS=0 -sSSLLIBDIR=/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/ -sSSLINCDIR=/usr/include/ p4` (for Release build)

